That's about the same as I found still with the good 'old' original gcc
on a 4.2 system for my 533 MHz LX when comparing FFTW (highly efficient
FFT routines) running times to the numbers somebody else published using
DECs c-compiler at maximum optimization: About 65-70% of DECs speed.
However, I did/could not yet try egcs 1.03 and see how much improvement
I probably get. At least for older versions of egcs I did NOT see ANY
improvement, I even checked the machine code it generated for the
critical inner loops and did see no change.

Nevertheless, those numbers you report are on about the same level I see
for typical, reasonably well-behaved problems on an Alpha compared to
Pentium II (I have a II-300 I did some tests on): 533 MHz Alpha under
Linux is probably equiv. to a (hypothetical) 600-700 MHz Pentium II
under Linux when using gcc. Not really bad as somebody report here, but
the performance/price ratio is not as good as you would like it.

Greetings

Joachim



