Robb <rr44607@swt.edu> writes:

> I have a newbie question concerning source RPMS. I run RedHat 5.0, and
> am trying to upgrade to the latest development kernel on my DEC XL300,
> and so need to upgrade a variety of packages. Most of the latest stuff
> is only available as .tgz
> or SRPMS. I have RTFM and whatnot, but cannot figure out the correct way
> to install a SRPM. Would someone be kind enough to give me the procedure
> and commands, or a pointer to some information?

Just "rpm -ihv <srpm>" and it will install the sources and any patches
in /usr/src/redhat/SOURCES, then you can build it there.

> 	Also, I have downloaded the latest egcs snapshot, and would like to
> install it as a secondary compiler. What is the method to install it
> without blasting away the existing gcc? What invocation to use when
> compiling with egcs? Again, instructions or a link would be much
> appreciated.

Just make sure you are installing it in a different place from the
regular gcc (like /usr/local/egcs/bin).  When you build the package,
just supply the "--prefix=/usr/local/egcs" (or whatever) flag to
configure.

egcs may have new options, which are documented in the egcs
distribution.  Otherwise, you use it the same as you would gcc.

Matt Grossman
mattg@oz.net
http://www.oz.net/~mattg



