I am a physicist too, and I have the same experience with Alpha/Linux---it
just isn't anywhere near as good as it should have been.

And yes, we use lots of basic transcendentals (sin/cos/exp) and complex
numbers.

Specifically, I simulate erbium-doped fiber ring-lasers for investigation
in a chaotic communications scheme.

Numerical optics is all about complex-valued envelopes. 

Indeed, I found a significant performance hit in going from Redhat 5.0 to
Redhat 5.1 on the x86.  Apparently in /usr/include/__math.h in 5.1 the
assembly inlines for sin/cos/exp have been deactivated with some note
claiming they are buggy.  Since we never found any significant problems
with them I edited the include file to reactivate it.

The 400 Mhz PII's seem to perform quite well, significantly better than
our 533Mhz 21164.   But I haven't yet installed the faster math lib yet
that was pointed to on this newsgroup. 

