The docs are available on the digital gatekeeper site.
Look around in gatekeeper.dec.com/...
I don't have all my references here(another office).

There are jumpers on the axppci motherboard for setting the
clock rates. I wondered whether or not they would really
have an effect on the chip. I don't have the axppci docs
here with me, but I fiddled around and watched the bogomips
on boot up to see what was happening. Sure enough, if I set
the jumpers to 166MHz, I got a rate of 165.9 or something
like that. Set the jumpers for 233MHz and I got 232.8 or
thereabout and with the jumpers set to 266, I get ~265.

So the proc was responding to the board jumpers. There was
also some fiddling with the cache and memory control
registers to reduce wait states and so on. That came later.
I cannot remember the fellow alpha-linuxer that was doing
most of the development of the tuning programs and I don't
have my email archives here.

The alpha box is OOS right now, but hopefully, I'll find
the time to bring it back to life before the DES2-2 contest
from RSA Labs starts up in a couple of weeks.





