<cbsmith@envisec.om> wrote:

>Paul Tomblin wrote in message <6mm5b3$45g$1@canoe.xcski.com>...
>>In a previous article, Nils Faerber <nils@unix-ag.org> said:
>>>Paul Tomblin <ptomblin@canoe.xcski.com> wrote:
>>>: Actually, it's usually called "True Parity", not ECC.  It can only

Typically the "true parity" refers to the fact that there is
actually data storage space available for all 36 bits. The
non-True parity memory devices discard the four extra bits
when written and re-generate the parity data from the data
as it is read. Obviously, this will defeat the ECC algorithms
employed by the motherboard logic, but it does save 1 bit out
of nine or so in silicon.

>detect, not
>>>: correct, errors, I believe.
>>>That's not completely true.
>>>The Alphas really use ECC as they use 36 bit SIMMs. For just parity 33
>bits
>>
>>36 bits gives 9 bits per byte, enough for one parity bit per byte.  I
>suppose
>>they *could* do limited error correction over the full 36 bits, but that's

All error detection and correction algorithms are 'limited'.
Even if you employ 64 bits to encode 32, you will still be
limited in the number and types of errors that can be detected
and/or corrected.

>not
>>the way I understand it works.
>From what I understand, 4 bits per 32 is sufficient for ECC algorithms.

36 bits for 32 can correct any 1 bit error in the 36 bits. It
can also detect any 2 bits in error in the 36. Results with 3
bit errors are basically undefined.

Realize that this is not just a set of 9 for 8 chips stuck
together end to end. It really is 36 for 32 as far as the
data encoding is concerned.




