Having asked a number of questions and read a lot of Digital
architectural documents we have found ways of making the 21164 processor
perform very well.

Currently we are using egcs 1.03a compiled for 21164 with Haifa
instruction reordering.  -O2 optimisation seems to do the best job with
our code whereas when using previous versions of gcc we were having to
do manual assembler tweaking and getting 30-40% speedup, now the tweaks
we have to do are pretty minimal and only gain about 10% over what egcs
is putting out.

You should look at your C code and try and maximise use of registers and
reduce loads and stores (this is basic stuff but quite often missed) and
if you really want to be using both pipelines you will need to worry
about things like logical shifts and stores which only go down specific
pipelines and you can often stall the processor if it all goes to pot.

While I think the 21264 processor will help a lot with bad code I don't
think we should just let the processor do all the work as a little extra
effort in coding will make a world of difference in performance on any
alpha, even my humble UDB.

-- 
