>>>>> "JS" == Joaquin Serna <joaquin@nauta.es> writes:

    JS> Hi. I�m interested to know the better hardware
    JS> configuration for an ISP to support Linux for web
    JS> services. Also if is possible the advantages and disadvantages
    JS> between NT and Linux.

If you're interested in Linux or NT on the Alpha (since you
cross-posted, I assume you are), Linux *seems* much faster than NT4 on
the same configuration.  I own an AlphaStation 200 4/233 with 64
Mbytes of RAM and around 6.6 Gbytes of disk space.  Under NT, the
system would swap a great deal, even when running singly, that is, no
other program was active other than system subprocesses, native
applications such as the C compiler (Visual C++ 5.0, RISC Edition) or
the web browser (Internet Explorer 3.01 Alpha).  Linux feels a lot
more responsive, load times are lower, and it is fairly stable
(although installation was difficult, and X crashed fairly regularly
until I upgraded the X server/device driver).  Unless you have special
needs, stick with a Linux distribution and a stable glibc and kernel.
From experience I can tell you that development-level code can be a
great headache---*not* something you need if you're trying to run a
business.

Linux is very good at networking---there's an ISP HOWTO listed on the
Linux Documentation Project home page---and purchasing a copy of Linux
is tons cheaper than purchasing a copy of NT Server.  RedHat Software
sells a secure web server package, which you might be interested in.
Craftworks Linux also sells a Linux/Alpha distribution, although I
haven't used it.  Debian GNU/Linux 2.0 is nearing release for the
Alpha platform; I'm considering switching to it (for purely political/
philosophical reasons; RedHat Linux is very good).

As far as hardware goes, you're not going to find very much that can
beat out the Alpha, performance-wise, although you should take into
consideration the kind and amount of load you're planning on (and you
should test any of your candidate systems in this fashion, to see if
they will suit your needs).  Anecdotal evidence (which is a kind of
oxymoron, I think) is really all I can offer you; you're best off on
setting up an NT system and a Linux system and try to compare the two,
side by side (same goes for hardware).

<flame>And next time, RTFM before you go posting to Usenet.  A simple
web search of "Linux ISP HOWTO" or "NT vs. UNIX" or "NT vs. Linux"
will probably get you a *lot* more (and higher quality)
information.</flame>

