we use linux around here for remote sensing R&D signal processing tasks.
most of our code is in Fortran (g77), and involves lots of multi-
dimensional complex FFT's with lots of complex multiplies/divides etc.
on very large data sets (hundreds or thousands of megabytes).  we have 
been using Dells (some PII/300's w/ 128M or 192M and a PPro/200 w/ 1G) 
for quite a while now, and are generally happy with them.  we just recently 
installed a Microway Screamer LX566 (w/ 512M, striped cheatahs, etc.; 
the works), and moved our codes over with great expectations.  we were fairly 
disappointed.  

it's close, but my codes run *faster* on the PII/300 than the Alpha/566.  
others around here are seeing similar results.

now i understand that we shouldn't expect g77 to perform at stellar levels
on the alpha yet, but that's what we are forced to use (Microway's NDP Fortran 
has some show-stopping limitations that keep us from being able to compile 
our codes with it).  

Microway told us "if you are doing Fortran, you probably want to use DU".
i believe it, but the DU licenses would cost about as much as the machine
(about $8k).  ugh.  i'd rather have two machines.

BUT... linux and g77 will only improve, and we'll be waiting.




