nospam@teflon.coated (Matt Nelson) writes:
> [...]
> we use linux around here for remote sensing R&D signal processing tasks.
> most of our code is in Fortran (g77), and involves lots of multi-
> dimensional complex FFT's [...]
> it's close, but my codes run *faster* on the PII/300 than the Alpha/566.  
> others around here are seeing similar results.

If you develop your code and tune it on x86, then run it with little
or no adaptation on Alpha, it's not surprising that the Alpha appears
to be only similar speed.

You could have done the opposite and found the Alpha three or four
times as fast.


The DEC compilers do a good job of munging code into suitable form for
Alpha, with lots of register usage, small-scale parallelism etc.

With GNU compilers, the object code is closer to a straight-through
translation of the source (no flames please :).

What this means is that you have to put in a bit of work to get top
performance.  Read a bit about the Alpha architecture, bear it in mind
when writing/tuning your code, and you WILL find the Alpha is
significantly faster, usually by 50% to 100%.


A quick solution to your problem: grab the FFTW routines for Alpha and
plug them in.  Whee!

Bye,
  Rob.



