>>
>>In addition i would like to know, how does the performance of Alpha
>>compare to Pentium II in real apps compiled on LINUX (with egcs)?
>>The spec values won't be reached by far i think. The problem is, 
>>that there are so many many more Intel boards running LINUX 
>>than Aplhas. So egcs-Intel should be far more optimized to Intel 
>>than to Aplha.
> 
> we use linux around here for remote sensing R&D signal processing tasks.
> most of our code is in Fortran (g77), and involves lots of multi-
> dimensional complex FFT's with lots of complex multiplies/divides etc.
> on very large data sets (hundreds or thousands of megabytes).  we have 
> been using Dells (some PII/300's w/ 128M or 192M and a PPro/200 w/ 1G) 
> for quite a while now, and are generally happy with them.  we just recently 
> installed a Microway Screamer LX566 (w/ 512M, striped cheatahs, etc.; 
> the works), and moved our codes over with great expectations.  we were fairly 
> disappointed.  

If you mainly work with FFT, the reason for your code running too
slow might be the glibc implementation of sin/cos. During the last
2 weeks, there were rumours, that someone provides a libfastm
(ported from the Cray routines) for these tasks. 
Its implementation is a lot faster.

Try searching the axp archive for cray or sin/cos fastmath...

Hope, that helps.
Martin.


