nospam@teflon.coated (Matt Nelson) wrote:

> we aren't a bunch of computer scientists; we're a bunch of physicists etc
> doing signal processing and data analysis.  we don't "tune" our code
> to the Intel platform.  we don't "tune" code at all (except for coding
> basics, like making sure nested loops are in the correct order, etc.).
> we generally don't have time for that - we're up to our ears just doing 
> the science.

Well, I'm a physicist too, and everything *I* throw at an Alpha runs at least 
twice as fast as on a P II.

Do you:

- Use transcendentals extensively (work is under way to improve the
  library functions - on the P II they're hardware instructions).
- Complex is broken in g77/gcc.  That's the reason g77 uses "emulation"
  of the complex type (i.e. the Fortran Frontend tells the backend what
  to do with the two values of the complex data item, instand of relying
  on the backends handling of the complex type).

Showing more of your code would give me more of an inkling what the problem 
is.

