>>>>> "tb" == theodore brace <brace@cyber1.servtech.com> writes:

    tb> Can anyone clue me in to why my date always is off by exactly
    tb> 30 years in linux?  I set the date from arc, and it says 2018
    tb> in linux.  I fix it from linux, and arc says the date is not
    tb> set and I have to set it.  Any ideas?  Oh, this is an
    tb> Alphastation 4/200 233 (yeah, another onsale sucker).

Do one (1) of the following:

	- Run a 2.1 kernel; there's code in the later development
	  kernels that auto-detects the clock's starting epoch and
	  compensates accordingly.

    OR

	- Run timeconfig and make certain the system clock is set to
	  ARC console time.

    OR

	- Run the command 'clock -A ...' (adding other options as
	  appropriate in the place of the "...") in your rc.local
	  startup script (or by hand each time you boot).

The second item above is the least invasive, but will only work if
you're running RedHat Linux.

