 Okay, Here are the details:
 The 25 MHz Power chip (as every POWER chip) can execute two
 floating point operations (one add and one multiply) per cycle.
 The Pentium can do one FP-operation.
 This gives a peak performance of 50 MFlops on the IBM
 and 300 MFlop on the Pentium.

 The POWER CHIP has 32 kbytes cache, the Pentium 512 kBytes.
    The LaPack package does mainly blocked matrix*matrix
 multiplies and gives 25 MFlop on the IBM ( 1/2 Peak )
 and about 33 MFlop on the Pentium.
    In both cases I used the unmodified sources,
 (no assembler BLAS)
 compiled them on the IBM with the IBM-compiler (high optimizations)
 and on the Pentium II with the NAG-f90 compiler (high optimization).

  I use both these machines every day and see that the pentium
 performs normally about four times as fast as this obsolete IBM,
 **but** for heavy number crunching, my IBM's perform better.
    I have made extensive optimization studies with the
 IBM-compiler, and have found that he can compile my inner loop
 such that the execution needs 19 cycles for 21 floating
 operations.
    ---> So, the speed of this old Lady is probably
 because the compiler is so extraordinary good.
  
