>  I use both these machines every day and see that the pentium
> performs normally about four times as fast as this obsolete IBM,
> **but** for heavy number crunching, my IBM's perform better.
>    I have made extensive optimization studies with the
> IBM-compiler, and have found that he can compile my inner loop
> such that the execution needs 19 cycles for 21 floating
> operations.
>    ---> So, the speed of this old Lady is probably
> because the compiler is so extraordinary good.

Getting a multiply-add in 2 cycles from the Pentium is extremely
difficult because while the multiplier can give one result per cycle,
there is 3 cycles of latency.  Take a look at the vector dot product
assembly code (based on code by Harlan Stockman) in Michael Schmit's
book on Pentium performance optimization to see what kinds of lengths
one must go through to get maximum throughput.  You have to keep track
of what is happening at every stage of all three of the Pentium's
pipelines (two integer and one floating point) in order to overlap
operations most efficiently (never stall a pipeline because it's
waiting for another pipeline's result), plus arrange the code very
carefully in order to not run out of registers.  This is far too much
to expect of any compiler.  It's much simpler on the RS/6000, which
has a MAC instruction and plenty of registers.

Nonetheless, if you're doing a lot of numerical linear algebra or
signal processing on a Pentium, you can get a huge speedup by using
assembly code like that on the inner loops.  You might want to modify
your matrix multiplication (etc.) routines to use inline assembly or
make function calls to do the row/column operations.



