Well, here are a few `real life' figures -- benchmarks for PARI-GP,
a computer algebra package  (which uses almost exclusively integer
operations).  From http://pari.home.ml.org/relv/current/MACHINES ;
smaller numbers in the `sta:' and `dyn:' columns mean the thing runs
in less time:

---8<---
Arch / proc:           OS:           Configure:     sta:    dyn:  GP version: 
==============================================================================
DEC/Alpha
alpha21164   600MHz    OSF1  T4.0    cc  X11 dl2    5930    6137   2.0.4k
alpha21164   500Mhz    Linux 2.0.30  gcc X11 dl2    6534    4062   2.0.8
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IBM/POWER,PowerPC
*PPC604e     233MHz    AIX-4.2       cc  X11   -   10786       -   2.0.8  (3)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Intel/x86
Pentium Pro  200MHz    Linux 2.0.27  gcc X11 dl2   12088   12214   2.0.4k
Pentium      266Mhz    Linux 2.0.29  gcc X11 dl2    9580    9575   2.0.7 (10)
Pentium      200Mhz    Nextstep 3.3  cc    -   -   16035       -   2.0.6  (1)
Pentium      166MHz    Solaris 2.6   gcc X11 dl2   18208   18342   2.0.8
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--->8---
(Skipping quite a few other combinations here.)

The Alphas outrun easily everything else tested so far, and there's not
much difference between cc under OSF and gcc under Linux.

(The line marked (10) is either a Pentium II or a Pentium MMX;  whoever
ran the benchmark on that didn't tell the maintainers of the list. ;)

What's remarkable about the PPC is that it keeps up with the others even
although it runs a version of the program entirely written in C -- the
other architectures use assembler routines for the most critical code
sections.  That PPC version was compiled with the native AIX cc by yours
truly;  the code produced by gcc was a lot slower on the same machine.

Enjoy, Gerhard "Shall we call that `room for improvement'."
