> not nearly as fast as the SPEC numbers had previously led him to believe.
> So that Alpha user's conclusion was that really there was NO extra bang
> for the buck with an Alpha system.

From the point of view of gcc / egcs, there are two *major* problems with 
Pentia:

1. Too few registers (gcc / egcs has all sorts of "special" code to deal
   with paucity of registers of the ix86 "architecture").

2. Double precision data not aligned on 8-byte addresses is awfully slow
   to access.  Unfortunately, Intel didn't realise that when they drew up
   the A(pplication) B(inary) I(nterface), so gcc/egcs has a hard time
   coping with this misfeature.

Of course, if your code happens to mostly consist of small basic blocks 
(small pieces of codes surrounded by jumps) and is not using any double 
precision data anyway, you won't run into these limitations.  In these cases 
(especially if it's also true that your code does a lot of character 
operations, like copying and comparing strings), you're probably not better 
of using an Alpha.

If, however, your programs are more like the average Fortran program, with 
five-page long do loops [sorry, couldn't resist] using lots of double 
precision calculations, an Alpha might be a nice choice.

