Michael Rich wrote:
> 
> Or is it just 32-bit?  I know it will run on an Alpha, and seeing that they
> are typically 64-bit, I was wonder if Linux was.

Linux is 64 bit on the processors like Alpha, Sparc, MIPS etc. Linux is
32 bit 
on other processors like i386/i486/Pentium and M68k.

> If it is, then Red hat must have a time keeping both the x86 and alpha
> versions in sync.

The Linux kernel code is maintained by "our leader" Linus Torvalds.
Redhat 
just maintains a Linux distribution (kernel + toolset + applications + 
installation procedure).

The Linux kernel source code has a small amount (less than 1%) of
processor 
specific code for all of the supported processors plus the general code
which 
is used on all processors. The code is written in such a way as to make
sure
it will compile cleanly for both 32 bit and 64 bit processors. It
theory, it 
would not be too difficult to port the code to a 128 bit processor when
one 
becomes available.

Unfortunately, many apps written for Intel Linux will not compile
correctly
for 64 bit processors due to programming naughties like casting a
pointer
to an int etc. It is however perfectly possible to write software which
compiles correctly for both 32 and 64 bit processors.

Hope this clears things up,
Erik



