On Wed, 17 Jun 1998, Courageous wrote:

> Warner Bruns wrote:
> > 
> >  I am using a predessor of the PPC, a 25 MHz POWER CHIP
> >  in a IBM-RS6000 320H. The machine was built may 1990.
> >  The "old Lady" is 2/3 the speed of a PII-300 MHz when running
> >  LaPack (a linear algebra package).
> 
> I find this *extraordinarily* difficult to believe...
> how many instructions does this 25 mhz power chip
> actually complete per clock cycle? how superscalar
> is the chip? and what kind of bandwidth/backplane
> does the workstation have?
> 
> Is you LaPack test primarily CPU-bound, or primarily
> bandwidth-bound?

AFAIK, this kind of library uses *heavily* floating point
operations... something the Pentium* family isn't very good
at, especially when compared to high end microprocessors
such as the Power* family. Don't believe it ? Have a look
at this URL :

  http://www.microprocessor.sscc.ru/microprocessor/

There is a comparison of different kinds of processors
(using SPEC95 benchmarks), and you will see that an IBM
Power2 at 160 MHz can really outperform a PII-400, and
even an Alpha 21164PC (and it is not far from the 21164
at 600 MHz). I have never used such processors, but it
looks like they are somewhat optimized for floating point
operations...

> The reason I'm a bit baffled here is that even if
> the PII is only completing a measely *1* instruction
> per clock cycle, that's still 300 million instructions
> per second. From a completely naive point of view, this
> 25 mhz POWER processor would have to have 12-way
> superscalar sustained execution in order to compare.

No, the PII doesn't execute 300 million instructions per
second... Some instructions can be executed in a single
cycle (eg, simple memory instructions or adds), sometimes
you're lucky enough to be able to execute two at a time,
but floating ops are much more complex and require
several cycles (sometime tens, or even hundreds) to be
completed. On the other hand, optimized processors can
use cycles much better. Sure, a Pentium is superscalar
and pipelined, but it doesn't compare well with high end
microprocessors. Don't forget it is cheap (thanks to mass
production, I guess), much cheaper than those little
wonders you find in bigger computers. Or isn't there an
exception to this called... "Alpha" ? :-)


Jean-Marc Saffroy




