Subject: How to get rid of unaligned traps (finally)
Date: 1998/06/12
Message-ID: <3581fbf8.5003264@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 362012849
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation, Palo Alto, CA, USA
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


People,
Like most of you, I'm annoyed to hell of the pesky unaligned traps
which occurs with certain commands/utilities (I'm running RH5.1 on an
AS200 4/166)...

It turns out, that on 64bit Alpha systems, memory must be aligned (see
pg 8 of Linux/Alpha FAQ); i.e. memory accesses (read & write) must be
in multiples -> you cannot declare a longword (Alpha jargon for 64bit
quantity), and then read it to a quadword (Alpha for 32bit). Example
of a longword is "long" and example of a quadword is "int".

But the kernel is flexible enough to accomodate that (how exactly it
is being done is at the black magic level and as I'm not a kernel guru
I would not be able to shed any light on that this :-( ...
Thus, the offending program/utility will _STILL_ run but the kernel
will be impacted by varying degrees on performance degradation...

Commenting out the printk statements from the kernel and upping the
syslog level is just cosmetic, the kernel still gets impacted. If
you're like me using _JUST_ a 166MHz system, you'd also want to milk
all the performance that you can get.

Netstat is a very common culprit of "unaligned traps", even the one
which came with RH5.1 !!

**** This version of netstat came with RH5.1 ****
# netstat -V
Linux NET-3 Base Utilities
Source: net-tools 1.33 net-tools@lina.inka.de (Bernd Eckenfels)
Kernelsource: 2.0.30
netstat 1.23 (1997-05-19)
Fred Baumgarten <dc6iq@insu1.etec.uni-karlsruhe.de> and Alan Cox.
+NEW_ADDRT +RTF_IRTT +RTF_REJECT +FW_IPMASQUERADE -NLS
AF:(inet) +UNIX +INET +IPX +AX25 +NETROM +ATALK
HW: +ETHER +ARC +SLIP +PPP +TUNNEL +TR +AX25 +NETROM +FR

**** This is what was logged ****
Jun 11 18:33:47 nexus kernel: netstat(878): unaligned trap at
00000200001615fc:
000000011ffff824 2d 10
Jun 11 18:33:47 nexus kernel: netstat(878): unaligned trap at
00000200001615fc:
000000011ffff834 2d 10
Jun 11 18:33:47 nexus kernel: netstat(878): unaligned trap at
00000200001615fc:
000000011ffff824 2d 10
Jun 11 18:33:47 nexus kernel: netstat(878): unaligned trap at
00000200001615fc:
000000011ffff834 2d 10

The unaligned traps will be solved with a combination of the latest
version of compilers/loaders. This implies that any libraries used
must also be compiled with these compilers.

Assuming you're running RH5.1 (comes with egcs-1.0.2), upgrade your
compiler to egcs-1.0.3a and you're basically done. This is as RH5.1
binutils are the latest.

So grab the latest source of your offending program/utility and
rebuild it using your newly installed egcs and these pesky "unaligned
traps" messages will be a thing of the past and your kernel will be
running at full speed.

As an aside, I grabbed the latest copy of net-tools and compiled it
using egcs-1.0.3a, and VOILA! NO MORE UNALIGNED TRAPS!!!

**** This is the latest and greatest net-tools ****
# ./netstat -V
net-tools 1.45
netstat 1.26 (1998-03-02)
Fred Baumgarten <dc6iq@insu1.etec.uni-karlsruhe.de> and Alan Cox.
+NEW_ADDRT +RTF_IRTT +RTF_REJECT +RT_NETLINK -FW_MASQUERADE -NLS
AF:(inet) +UNIX +INET -INET6 +IPX -AX25 -NETROM +ATALK
HW: +ETHER -ARC -SLIP +PPP -TUNNEL -TR -AX25 -NETROM -FR

Good Hunting!
May you never see unaligned traps again!
HTH,
Wai-Sun



