Use a linux boot/emergency disk to fsk your hdd's. In the
future, avoid if you can hitting the reset button on a
unix-based machine. It's not good for it.

RH5 installs ctrl-alt-delete as "shutdown -now", which
is safe; assuming your kernel is still running, that is.
Take a peek at /etc/inittab to see if anything is there
for ctrl-alt-delete. If there is, next time use that and
not the reset button. If there isn't, you'll need to get
it there. Careful with inittab, though. Put the 
wrong thing in and your machine won't boot.



Joe.



