Courageous wrote:
> 
> >Right now, no. A 533 mhz alpha is SLOWER than a 400 mhz pentium
> > >at int operatiosn, and only about 15% faster for fp.
> >
> > What?!  The 21164LX 533MHz beats the PII 400 in int and fp operations.  And
> > the 21164SX 533MHz is faster in fp, close in int, and half the cost.  Also,
> > you can pick up the 21164LX at near the same cost as similarly configured
> > Intel system.  And getting nearly twice the speed in fp more than justifies
> > the small increase in cost.
> >
> > SpecINT
> > PII 400 = 15.3
> > Alpha 21164LX 533MHz = 16.6
> > Alpha 21164SX 533MHz = 12.4
> >
> > SpecFP
> > PII 400 = 12.0
> > Alpha 21164LX 533MHz = 21.9
> > Alpha 21164SX 533MHz = 16.1
> 
> That's funny. I'm not sure where you're getting your figures,
> but I got mine from www.alphapowered.com, which is digital's
> own website. IT says, int95 = 12.7, fp95 = 14.1. This would
> put the pII 400 in the integer lead, and the alpha ahead of
> the PII 400 in fp by less than 15%. Like I said. It didn't
> say what mobo they used, but I would ordinarily trust Dec
> to show their processor in the best possible light........
>

Ignoring for the moment the question what really comes out of a typical
application, especially under Linux, because the old math function
routine here really were slow, but the situation already has improved
with the cray subroutines and I already have better *fully free*
routines for the trig functions that are again nearly twice as fast as
crays (to be realeased soon), Mark is right, at least, depends what kind
of 21164 your are refering to.

The numbers from alphapowered.com refer to the 21164*PC* version, which
is a stripped down cheapo version of the original 21164 as it is in a
current 21164LX board. The latter one really delivers the higher number
Mark quotes.

However, being a somewhat disappointed Alpha user myself, overall
price/power ratio was not too good for our complete SCSI 64Mb/4G 533LX
system that we bought for about $5500 bucks st the beginning of this
year. I think you can get pretty much cheaper deals now, especially in
the states, <where everything is better> (??).

Joachim



