>>>>> "RP" == Randall Parker <rgparker@west.net> writes:

    RP> A friend tells me that DEC Unix and NT can't be made to
    RP> install to different disk partitions on the same Alpha system
    RP> because they require incompatible BIOS's.

    RP> Can Linux use the same BIOS as DEC Unix?

    RP> Or the same BIOS as Alpha NT?

    RP> ie is there a way to have a dual booting system with Linux and
    RP> NT? Or Linux and DEC Unix?

Life is complicated on the Alpha.  :)

If you like, you can install NT, Digital UNIX, *and* Linux on a single
Alpha.  NT and Digital UNIX *must* be installed to different fixed
disk drives, but Linux happily shares disks with both OSes.

Linux can boot directly from SRM (which is what I recommend using to
boot), or it can be booted from MILO, which can be started from
*either* ARC or SRM.

If your system has room for both kinds of firmware in flash, you can
switch back and forth among Linux, Digital UNIX, and NT pretty
easily.  If not, you can run ARC from the firmware CD; read the
firmware release notes for the gory details.

