I finally got aboot installed properly on my AS 200. Thanks to all who
gave hints.

In case someone else may find this useful, here are the details:

- I deleted all standard fdisk partitions from the disk. (I'm not
certain this made a difference.)

- I stopped trying to use minlabel, and used the fdisk option b instead.
(This was my final hurdle.)

- I deleted the default partitions a: and c:, and created my own
partitions a: and b:, leaving no gaps, no partitions of type 'unused',
etc. (This was my second-to-last hurdle.)

- I used the following command to install aboot (after booting using the
kernel on the floppy and the root partition on the hard disk):
# swriteboot /dev/sda /boot/boot1x
Installing the kernel with aboot is unnecessary for my system.
(When I installed RedHat Linux, selecting "install everything", the
aboot package was installed.)

- My boot command is as follows:
>>> b dka0 -fi 2/vmlinux.gz -fl root=/dev/sda2
Of course, I've stored this information in environment variables
bootdef_dev, boot_file and boot_osflags. I actually use:
>>> b

Here is my partition information.

bytes/sector: 512
sectors/track: 62
tracks/cylinder: 67
sectors/cylinder: 4154
cylinders: 1009
2 partitions:
#        size   offset    fstype   [fsize bsize   cpg]
  a:   265856     4154      swap                        # (Cyl.    2 -
65)
  b:  3921376   270010      ext2                        # (Cyl.   66 -
1009)

Here my waste using the disk geometry set up by Linux/Alpha fdisk is
less than the waste on Englehard's disk, which appears to use a geometry
set up on a PC with extended bios translation. Linux fdisk will
configure using its preferred geometry if fdisk is restarted with no
partitions defined.

Randy



