I presume the "unknown instruction" error is the well known
__builtin_memset error.
I've compiled 2.1.102 to 2.1.105 using egcs, and although it is not
recommended, I don't seem to have come across any other problems (yet).

As far as the __builtin_memset stuff is concerned, you should be able to
get the kernel to compile with the following patch.

--- linux/include/asm-alpha/string.h.orig       Fri Apr 10 06:25:33 1998
+++ linux/include/asm-alpha/string.h    Thu Apr  9 19:28:23 1998
@@ -24,7 +24,8 @@
 extern void * __constant_c_memset(void *, unsigned long, long);
 extern void * __memset(void *, char, size_t);

-#if __GNUC__ > 2 || __GNUC_MINOR__ >= 8
+/* #if __GNUC__ > 2 || __GNUC_MINOR__ >= 8 */
+#if 0
 #define memset(s, c, n)
    \
 (__builtin_constant_p(c)                                                   \
  ? (__builtin_constant_p(n) && (c) == 0
    \



This seems to have been fixed in 2.1.105, where these lines now look like:

#if __GNUC__ > 2 || __GNUC_MINOR__ >= 91
#define memset(s, c, n)                                                     \
(__builtin_constant_p(c)                                                    \




