Hi Tony,

Here's a brief summary of how I loaded Redhat onto my Aspen Durango last 
winter (I wrote down the steps I used in my logbook which I'm looking
at now.)

1. I created 3 floppies using a PC and the redhat CD. From a DOS shell
   I executed "rawrite" to create:

	- Milo Disk:  this disk contains the milo image and linload.exe
	- Kernel Disk
	- Ramdisk

	C:\> D:\dosutils\rawrite
	     image source file: D:\milo\images\pc164.img
	     target diskette:	A:
	     insert formatted diskette into drive A:
	and so on....

   You'll need a different image file for the Aspen Alpine (pc64?)

2. Next I started up the machine, and from the ARC console I selected
"F2" 	
   and specified a boot option from the floppy "A:" drive and removed
all     
   boot options.

3. I inserted the Milo floppy and booted up using the new boot option,
   this gave me the MILO> prompt

4. I put in the kernel floppy and issued the boot command from the MILO
   prompt:

MILO> boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1 prompt_ramdisk=1

   this starts loading the kernel and prompts you for the ramdisk
floppy.
   If the ramdisk loads and everything goes okay then you will see the
   first installtion screen come up. You will be prompted to partition
   your disk, customize the install and so on. When the install is done
   you will restart your system. At this point I reinserted the MILO
   floppy and restarted the system.

5. On reboot I again came to the MILO prompt and this time I specified
   booting from the new system and I choose to start in single user
mode:

MILO> boot sda2:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda2 single

   This brings up a #bash prompt. Booting in single user mode allowed
   me to put off dealing with NFS stuff until later. Also note that
   I have my root partition as the second primary partition, the first
   is a small msdos partition for milo.


6. This is when I copied the milo and linload executables of the floppy
   to my small msdos partition

	- Make sure MILO floppy is in the floppy drive
	- mount it 

	#bash mkdir /mnt1
	#bash mount -t msdos /dev/fd0 /mnt1
	
	- mount the small msdos partition:

	#bash mkdir /mnt2
	#bash mount -t msdos /dev/sda1 /mnt2


	- copy the contents of the floppy to the disk:

	#bash cd /mnt2
	#bash cp /mnt1/* .
	#bash ls
	  linload.exe milo

7. At this point I shutdown the system so I could edit the boot config
   in ARC. At this point I added a new boot configuration "Linux" using 
   "disk0 partition1" with linload.exe as the osloader.	See the
section   
   "Loading MILO from the Windows NT ARC firmware" in the redhat manual
   for more details on how to specify your boot configuration.

   Once this is done you can boot up the machine without floppies.


Anyway, I hope this helps and doesn't contain too many errors. 

-Tim


