Well, it nearly happened yesterday, after 6 months on the road I nearly
had the big one. I commute everyday to work, up and down Port Wakefield Rd
in Adelaide. For those who dont know this road, it is a dual lane
80/90kmh  devided arterial, that passes through industrial estates. The
prob with this road is that it has heaps of side streets coming onto it,
prime places for cagers not to see you coming down at 90 kmh as they turn
across you. Now I know were these side streets are, and everyday someone
times it just right to cross you, so I'm already warey. But yesterday a
person coming from a left sidestreet appears at the last moment from
behind a set of trees at high velocity, obviously trying to time it across
me, I see this and grab a handleful of brake, she oops! I mean the person
decides they cant make it and pulls up half across my lane. I miss by
about 2 foot doing approx 80 k. I'm not afraid to say but I nearly soiled
my pants, and couldnt wait to get home and off the bike. The scariest
thing was, every day Im expecting it, Im waiting for it, it finally happens
and there was still stuff all I could  do except brake/swerv/hoipe for the
best. Why didnt we hit, may be it was late afternoon and she saw the
headlight at the last moment, who knows, luck?. Scarey stuff, but I'll be
back on the bike next week (badly sprained ankle at basketball that night,
shouldnt have got out of bed). Just a human interest storey to pass on, no
real moral except to maintain awareness of your surroundings whilst on the
road.
--
Geoff Murdoch (GPZ250R)



