There is a very good document on the RH home page called "Introduction
to Alpha Systems and Processors". It covers the basics of what you
need to know. Right now, I wouldn't recommend an alpha based system,
unless you were going to be using applications that took advantage of
it's strengths (fast FPU, 64 bits) for a couple reasons.
1) Unless you intend on spending a lot of time altering code, you
might not be able to compile and run some of your favorite programs.
No gaurantees either way, but C code that specifies 32 bit longs won't
nessesarily compile unless they are changed to 64 bits.
2) Intel socket 7 cpu's are Soooooo dirt cheap right now, if you only
have 800 dollers, it's hard to recommend anything other then upgrading
the processor and a getting great SCSI system. With a little bit of
reasearch, a good SCSI system and a good monitor/graphics card combo
will carry to your next computer, wheater it's an alpha or a PC. So
what if it's not the latest and greatest..... what in the WORLD are
you going to use a Pentium II for? You've already got a good operating
system, and that's most of the battle. ftp.cdrom.com serves like 3000
simultainous FTP connections on like a Pentium Pro 200 (or overclocked
233...). Granted it has a gig of ram, but still... a little (lot at
~$1.25/meg) more ram and a good SCSI system will serve you well.

So, the answer to your question is, Right now DEC's don't compare
particularly well to intel processors in the price/perfomace ratio,
unless you are willing to spend a BUCKET load of money for a processor
that runs faster then pentium IIs will for a long time.
In terms of SPARCS and SGIs, I personally would not be inclined to use
them right away because it seems like the ports for them are still
relatively new.

My two Cents...

Jesse
